    'styles_used': list([
      'Heading 1',
      'Heading 2',
      'Heading 3',
      'List Bullet',
      'List Bullet 2',
      'Normal',
      'RichdocCode',
    ]),
    'tables': list([
      dict({
        'n_cols': 1,
        'n_rows': 1,
        'rows': list([
          list([
            '''
              TL;DR
              A conversion is persisted as two rows (FROM side + TO side) that share a conversion_id UUID. Both rows carry signed integer delta_cases equal in absolute value. New enum item_conversion_role. New tables app_data.unsaved_item_conversions (per-user, versioned) and app_data.saved_item_conversions (per-timeframe), plus a latest_unsaved_item_conversions view mirroring the existing pattern. New table item_relationships.supc_projected_opco_metrics holds per-opco projected metrics for TO items missing from master. New nullable column cross_purchase_ratio on item_relationships.item_pair_relationships. All in one Liquibase changelog: 020-item-conversion-tables.xml.
            ''',
          ]),
        ]),
      }),
      dict({
        'n_cols': 1,
        'n_rows': 1,
        'rows': list([
          list([
            '''
              Naming conventions (verified on main)
              Primary keys: pk_<table> Foreign keys: fk_<base_table>_<referenced_table> Indexes: idx_<table>_<columns> Unique constraints: uq_<table>_<columns> Check constraints: ck_<table>_<short_predicate> Liquibase changelogs: NNN-feature-name.xml, three-digit zero-padded sequence. Next slot: 020-item-conversion-tables.xml. Enum types live in their owning schema (per the convention established by 016-relocate-enum-types-to-owning-schemas.xml).
            ''',
          ]),
        ]),
      }),
      dict({
        'n_cols': 1,
        'n_rows': 1,
        'rows': list([
          list([
            '''
              What 'cross_purchase_ratio on the conversion record' is for
              The conversion record carries a snapshot of the ratio at the moment the conversion was made. The live ratio lives on item_relationships.item_pair_relationships and is refreshed by the (future) Redshift job. Snapshotting on the conversion lets us answer questions like "how often do users override the system's suggestion?" and "what did the data say at the time the merchandiser decided?" — purely audit / analytics, not for math.
            ''',
          ]),
        ]),
      }),
      dict({
        'n_cols': 2,
        'n_rows': 11,
        'rows': list([
          list([
            'conversion_id',
            'UUID generated by the API on conversion create. Identical on both FROM and TO rows of the same conversion. Survives the unsaved → saved promotion.',
          ]),
          list([
            'role',
            'Enum FROM or TO. Each conversion has exactly one row of each role per (conversion_id, version).',
          ]),
          list([
            'version',
            'Starts at 1. The user editing a conversion appends a new pair of rows with version + 1; old versions are retained for audit. The latest_unsaved_item_conversions view exposes only the highest version.',
          ]),
          list([
            'timeframe_id',
            "FK to app_data.timeframes. The conversion is tied to a timeframe because per-case metrics, choice groups, and the user's simulation context are all timeframe-scoped.",
          ]),
          list([
            'username',
            'FK to app_data.users. Unsaved state is per-user.',
          ]),
          list([
            'opco_id',
            'No FK to master_data.opcos — consistent with how the existing schema handles opco references in app_data (string keys, logical reference).',
          ]),
          list([
            'supc',
            'Canonical SUPC string. Always populated, even when opco_itm_id is NULL (TO items missing from master_data). The supc value identifies the item universally; the opco_itm_id is just a master_data-side resolution.',
          ]),
          list([
            'opco_itm_id',
            "Nullable. Must be NOT NULL on FROM rows (you can only move volume that exists in opco_items), enforced by ck_..._from_has_id. NULL on TO rows when the TO item isn't stocked by the opco — projections come from supc_projected_opco_metrics instead.",
          ]),
          list([
            'delta_cases',
            'Signed integer. ck_..._sign enforces FROM < 0 and TO > 0. Equal absolute magnitude across the two rows of a single conversion is application-enforced (single INSERT statement / transaction).',
          ]),
          list([
            'sa_type',
            'Audit snapshot of which IRP relationship type the user picked (C, I, SA1). Nullable for cases where the conversion was free-text-entered without an IRP candidate (open question — see open questions).',
          ]),
          list([
            'cross_purchase_ratio',
            'Audit snapshot of what the live ratio said at conversion time. NUMERIC(5,4) for [0.0000, 1.0000] with bps precision. NULL when no ratio exists yet (v1 default, until Redshift job is wired up).',
          ]),
        ]),
      }),
      dict({
        'n_cols': 1,
        'n_rows': 1,
        'rows': list([
          list([
            '''
              Why composite PK and no surrogate id
              The natural identity of a row is (conversion_id, role, version). Both unsaved_changes (composite PK) and unsaved_simulations (surrogate id) patterns exist in app_data; composite suits conversions better because the components have meaning and queries frequently filter by (conversion_id, version).
            ''',
          ]),
        ]),
      }),
      dict({
        'n_cols': 1,
        'n_rows': 1,
        'rows': list([
          list([
            '''
              Should saved unsaved-history survive the save?
              Reasonable case for adding a saved_item_conversions_history table mirroring the existing saved_changes_history pattern (from 004-history-table-additions.xml). Deferred to open questions — depends on whether anyone actually needs "show me every edit Alice made to this conversion before she saved it".
            ''',
          ]),
        ]),
      }),
      dict({
        'n_cols': 1,
        'n_rows': 1,
        'rows': list([
          list([
            '''
              Info
              The Java side maps this view to an immutable read-only entity (mirroring LatestUnsavedChange.java). Repositories use it for "what does Alice's pending state look like" reads, and the save flow uses it to discover which rows to promote.
            ''',
          ]),
        ]),
      }),
      dict({
        'n_cols': 1,
        'n_rows': 1,
        'rows': list([
          list([
            '''
              A note on the COALESCE'd primary key
              Standard SQL doesn't allow expressions in PRIMARY KEY; PostgreSQL does support UNIQUE INDEX on expressions but not PK. Practical alternatives:
              (a) Use a non-NULL sentinel like opco_id = '' for network-level rows; PK is (supc, opco_id). (b) Use the PG-15+ NULLS NOT DISTINCT clause: UNIQUE NULLS NOT DISTINCT (supc, opco_id) as a unique constraint (not PK).
              v1 chooses (a) — empty string for network-level rows. It's portable and reads cleanly. The doc'd PK above is illustrative; the real migration uses opco_id VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL DEFAULT '' with a normal composite PK.
            ''',
          ]),
        ]),
      }),
      dict({
        'n_cols': 2,
        'n_rows': 6,
        'rows': list([
          list([
            'supc',
            'SUPC string. Always populated. Soft reference to supc_store.supc.',
          ]),
          list([
            'opco_id',
            "Empty string for network-level (T3) rows, otherwise the opco identifier. The application layer maps '' ↔ NULL at read time so the rest of the codebase sees a clean nullable.",
          ]),
          list([
            'projected_per_case_gp / projected_per_case_net_sales',
            'Projected per-case GP and net sales. Same unit as opco_items.gross_prft / cases etc.',
          ]),
          list([
            'projected_gross_prft_vlcty',
            'Projected weekly GPV — used for parity with the existing opco_items.gross_prft_vlcty field (and downstream consumers that may need it).',
          ]),
          list([
            'projection_source',
            "Enum identifying which tier produced the projection. Drives the UI's confidence badge.",
          ]),
          list([
            'last_refreshed_at',
            'When the daily ETL last computed this row. Helps surface stale projections after a master-data refresh (see Q4 in open questions).',
          ]),
        ]),
      }),
      dict({
        'n_cols': 1,
        'n_rows': 1,
        'rows': list([
          list([
            '''
              Decision: compute in the service layer, not as a SQL view
              A natural design instinct is a SQL view effective_opco_items. We rejected it for v1 because:
              PostgreSQL views can't take parameters; "for this user" would force a parameterised function instead, complicating querying. The math is trivial — opco_items.cases + Σ(deltas) — and the inputs are already fetched for other reasons (the UI loads pending changes for the panel anyway). Keeping it in the service layer lets us add policy (e.g. "what if user X is reviewing user Y's saved state?") without DDL changes.
            ''',
          ]),
        ]),
      }),
      dict({
        'n_cols': 1,
        'n_rows': 1,
        'rows': list([
          list([
            '''
              Why one changelog file
              The six changes form one logical migration ("introduce item conversion persistence"). Rolling out partial state (e.g. tables without the view) would leave the API in a broken intermediate. Liquibase still permits per-changeSet rollback within the file.
            ''',
          ]),
        ]),
      }),
      dict({
        'n_cols': 3,
        'n_rows': 6,
        'rows': list([
          list([
            '',
            'Query pattern',
            'Index used',
          ]),
          list([
            "Load Alice's pending conversions for timeframe 12",
            'idx_unsaved_item_conversions_user_timeframe(username, timeframe_id)',
            '',
          ]),
          list([
            'What conversions touch opco_itm_id 554321 (any user, any state)?',
            'idx_unsaved_item_conversions_opco_itm + idx_saved_item_conversions_opco_itm (partial WHERE NOT NULL)',
            '',
          ]),
          list([
            'Show all conversions affecting SUPC 0013060 in opco 042',
            'idx_unsaved_item_conversions_supc_opco + idx_saved_item_conversions_supc_opco',
            '',
          ]),
          list([
            'Saved conversions for timeframe 12 (e.g. simulation aggregate roll-up)',
            'idx_saved_item_conversions_timeframe',
            '',
          ]),
          list([
            'Latest version per conversion_id (used by view)',
            'Composite PK (conversion_id, role, version) serves as the index — DISTINCT ON walks it in reverse',
            '',
          ]),
        ]),
      }),
    ]),
  })
